export const body = "<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>Cel-shading is a rendering style designed to make 3D objects appear 2D. \"Cel\" is short for Celluloid, transparent plastic sheets used in traditional 2D animation; these \"cel\" then get painted on, placed on backgrounds, and sequentially photographed. When we playback these images (usually at 12 or 24 frames), a cartoon is made. Cel-shading is also known as \"Toon Shading\" since it produces cartoon-like graphics.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/fd7d40373792b81ac8a8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/fd7d40373792b81ac8a8.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"536\" style=\"--source-width:308px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/6233e70dfbc7106ff739.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/6233e70dfbc7106ff739.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"639\" style=\"--source-width:308px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/6ff3659c754bbe500093.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/6ff3659c754bbe500093.webp\" width=\"1068\" height=\"687\" style=\"--source-width:258px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><em>Jet Set Radio</em><em><strong>, </strong></em>released in 2000, is among the first games to feature cel-shaded graphics. To achieve this art style, the game used:</p>\n<ul><li>Bold outlines</li><li>Exaggerated shapes</li><li>Less gradient</li><li>Less shading color</li><li>Flat, bright colors</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><p>Over 20 years of development (since <em>Jet Set Radio</em>), many developers have taken a stab at cel-shading, but perhaps Arc System Works is the most prominent name.</p>\n<p><em>Guilty Gear</em> is their flagship series with beautiful sprite-based graphics, but after <em>Guilty Gear XX: Accent Core Plus R </em>(2012), they decided to refresh this series with 3D graphics. Cel-shading was their best choice to bring the 2D aesthetic to 3D.</p>\n<p>Guilty Gear Xrd not only inherited all characteristics of cel-shading but also made a big improvement on simulating 2D lighting. Each character in Guilty Gear Xrd has their own light sources that affect only their body and nothing else in the scene, exactly like 2D sprite graphics.</p>\n<p><em><br></em><em><br></em><em><br></em><em><br></em><em><br></em><em>Extra: If you are</em><em> interested</em><em> in more technical details of </em><em><strong>Guilty Gear Xrd</strong></em><em>, you can watch their sharing from GDC 2015.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/88914972732021eb976e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/88914972732021eb976e.webp\" width=\"1060\" height=\"587\" style=\"--source-width:258px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/8b3174beeb6d60100273.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/8b3174beeb6d60100273.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"524\" style=\"--source-width:308px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>The character's lighting is fixed no matter the environment they are in.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/0d40d7ca7f1a7f06f79e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/0d40d7ca7f1a7f06f79e.webp\" width=\"672\" height=\"355\" style=\"--source-width:308px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Later versions of the game offer the option of dynamic scene-based lighting.</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Some popular titles with cel-shaded graphics are:</p>\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>The <em>Borderlands </em>series</p></td><td><p><em>The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild </em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/98e60e3780dcd44ed91e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/98e60e3780dcd44ed91e.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"656\" style=\"--source-width:121px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/efa6f1eb18d553cb0081.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/efa6f1eb18d553cb0081.webp\" width=\"728\" height=\"410\" style=\"--source-width:121px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><em>Astral Chain</em></p></td><td><p><em>The Wolf Among Us 2</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/65187872dda61ef7a5f7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/65187872dda61ef7a5f7.webp\" width=\"632\" height=\"356\" style=\"--source-width:121px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/a38a92647eff1805ae30.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/a38a92647eff1805ae30.webp\" width=\"624\" height=\"355\" style=\"--source-width:121px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p>Cel-shading is a flexible art style that reminds us of the beauty of traditional 2D animation. Other than having the nostalgic hand-drawn quality, cel-shaded graphics also age gracefully. <em>The Wind Waker</em> and <em>Okami</em>, both over a decade old, are two shining examples, as they still look great by today's standard. (And they're still fun to play.)</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/1b6696f33429a1ccbc0e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/1b6696f33429a1ccbc0e.webp\" width=\"522\" height=\"261\" style=\"--source-width:308px\" alt=\"Illustration 11\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>The Legend of Zelda: The Wind Waker</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/fb7a228489d3f83ea4fd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/fb7a228489d3f83ea4fd.webp\" width=\"534\" height=\"275\" style=\"--source-width:308px\" alt=\"Illustration 12\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Okami</em></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>";
